A blocking call in a timer's `@tick` gets a goroutine like one anywhere else.

`offloadableFuncs` is the list of bodies passAsyncOffload may enter from the
target's own thread, and a tick has been missing from it twice, for two
different reasons.

It read each component's timers and the package's, and a window's vars, funcs
and error handler but not its timers -- and passTimerPrimitive records a
schedule on whichever owner held the node, so an inlined timer's is a window's.

Both timers here are an `effect` now, so the tick is a lambda handed to
`fynert.Every` or `gtk4rt.Every`, and a lambda is deliberately not an entry
point: what calls one is usually code this pass can read, and `xs.map(f)` wants
the blocking f rather than a goroutine per element. The exception is a callee
that *says* it schedules -- the `schedules` flag on its native mark -- because a
host scheduler calls its callback from the loop it owns, which is the thread the
target draws on.

The flag is asked for rather than read off the call, which is the rule the whole
of #[foreign] follows: a foreign declaration describes an identifier and does
not implement it, so nothing here can find out when the host runs an argument.
A scheduler whose declaration forgets it gets the old bug rather than a new one,
which is the first `deny` below firing.

The failure was silent both times, which is what makes it worse than the
ternary panic in `timer_tick_lowered.txtar`: the tick body was left as written,
so `os.Hostname()` ran on the thread the toolkit draws on -- fyne's own
goroutine, gtk4's main loop. A poll of anything slower than a hostname lookup
freezes the window, and the generated code compiles and runs.

The third thing this pins is what the lambda does *not* make async. Colouring
once read through a closure, so `handle = every(d, func(){ ...blocking... })`
was itself an async statement -- and passAsyncOffload refuses one inside the
`if` its caller wraps it in, for a mount that hands work over and blocks on
nothing. Constructing a closure is not calling it.

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `m.name__inst0 = func\(\) string \{ v, _ := os.Hostname` -- the answer is bound on the spawned goroutine and posted back, never assigned on the drawing thread
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `(?m)^\tgo func\(\)` -- the goroutine is inside the tick, not around the arming: a mount that hands a closure over blocks on nothing
deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `(?m)^\tgo func\(\)` -- likewise on the other Go platform that schedules with an effect
deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `m.name__inst0 = func\(\) string \{ v, _ := os.Hostname` -- likewise: the answer is bound on the spawned goroutine and posted back

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import time "sngl:time"
import go "sngl:language/go"

#[go.native("os", "os.Hostname", fails)]
#[go.async]
func host() string

output {
    go {
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
}

component poller ui.node {
    var name = "idle"

    time.timer(interval=50ms, @tick {
        name = host()
    })

    ui.text(value=name)
}

ui.window #main(title="poll") {
    poller()
}
